From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 11:08:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: let the core suspend after a reset

The reset path drops the runtime PM reference with
pm_runtime_put_noidle(), which does not let the device suspend, so the
power domain is never switched off and on again.

Use pm_runtime_put_autosuspend() instead, so the reset path handles
runtime PM the same way as a normal job completion.

Also drop the runtime PM reference after rocket_core_reset(), so the NPU
core stays powered during the reset.

Link: https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260819073530.6087-1-royalnet026@gmail.com/
Fixes: 0810d5ad88a1 ("accel/rocket: Add job submission IOCTL")
Signed-off-by: Jiaxing Hu <gahing@gahingwoo.com>
Reviewed-by: Heiko Stuebner <heiko@sntech.de>
Tested-by: Sidong Yang <sidong.yang@furiosa.ai> # RK3588, ROCK 5B+
Reviewed-by: Tomeu Vizoso <tomeu@tomeuvizoso.net>
[tomeu: dropped the outdated RK3576 rationale from above]
Signed-off-by: Tomeu Vizoso <tomeu@tomeuvizoso.net>
Link: https://patch.msgid.link/2afd051e37b8dd4f10e347a3537b0086973d554c.1794615323.git.gahing@gahingwoo.com
[carried for the RK3588 series as upstream 36161ff0c00f, rebased onto 088
 and 091. 088 detaches the IOMMU after the reset rather than before it, so
 the reference is put after that detach, which keeps the core powered for
 all of the hardware work the reset does. 091 is what guarantees no
 threaded handler is still running when the core becomes suspendable.
 Dropped when the base carries 36161ff0c00f.]
---
 drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c | 20 ++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
index ed090d8d73e..f9d00f27ad3 100644
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -496,6 +496,8 @@ static void rocket_job_handle_irq(struct rocket_core *core)
 static void
 rocket_reset(struct rocket_core *core, struct drm_sched_job *bad)
 {
+	bool put_job_ref;
+
 	if (!atomic_read(&core->reset.pending))
 		return;
 
@@ -569,14 +571,13 @@ rocket_reset(struct rocket_core *core, struct drm_sched_job *bad)
 	synchronize_irq(core->irq);
 
 	/*
-	 * No handler is running now, but we might still have stuck jobs. Let's
-	 * make sure the PM counters stay balanced by manually calling
-	 * pm_runtime_put_noidle().
+	 * No handler is running now, but we might still have stuck jobs. Take
+	 * the job's runtime PM reference over from it, and keep it until the
+	 * core has been reset and its IOMMU detached: the reference is what
+	 * keeps the power domain up while that happens.
 	 */
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) {
-		if (core->in_flight_job)
-			pm_runtime_put_noidle(core->dev);
-
+		put_job_ref = core->in_flight_job;
 		core->in_flight_job = NULL;
 	}
 
@@ -602,6 +603,13 @@ rocket_reset(struct rocket_core *core, struct drm_sched_job *bad)
 		core->attached_domain = NULL;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Nothing below touches the hardware, so balance the PM counters now,
+	 * and request idle while doing it so the core can suspend.
+	 */
+	if (put_job_ref)
+		pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(core->dev);
+
 	/* NPU has been reset, we can clear the reset pending bit. */
 	atomic_set(&core->reset.pending, 0);
 
-- 
